## Before you write code

If this slice turns on an architecture question — a decision neither the
brief nor a Done slice in the milestone actually settles — do not guess
and start writing: raise it with the user right away, before code, not at
hand-back where an hour of the wrong shape is already sunk. And never run
`complete-slice` on work whose architecture is still unsettled that way —
a hand-back is a claim the shape is right, not a place to flag that it
might not be.

## Do the work

Work in the working directory above; if that is not where this session
started, use absolute paths or `git -C`. Honour the brief's acceptance
criteria and the project's verification gate before calling it done.

Read files with the Read tool, not `cat`/`sed`/`head` — Read handles
offsets for files too big to read whole. Edit files with Edit or Write,
not a shell heredoc — a heredoc edit re-transmits the whole old block and
the whole new one, and this codebase's house style is dense enough prose
that doubling it on every touch adds up fast. The shell is for running
things — tests, git, the verification gate — not for reading or editing
files.

If the work is code: branch for the slice — one branch, and exactly ONE
change on it — commit, and push the branch. Do not run `gh`, and do not
open a pull request: you hand the branch back and the user opens the
pull request from the board once they have reviewed it.

If the work is not code — docs, research, written-up findings — produce
the deliverable the brief asks for and link it in the summary below.

## Finish

Work you noticed but did not do — a bug beside your change, a test gap
in code you didn't touch, a refactor the brief didn't ask for — is not
yours to do and not yours to lose. When the gate is green, before
`complete-slice`, hand each one in and **stop**:

    nat slice-followups 3b738308-f654-8170-8c99-eccab4463d8f --project 3b738308-f654-8117-9f2e-c3b0a9b7f001 \
        --follow-up '<title line>

<two or three sentences: what, where, why it matters>'

`--follow-up` repeats, one per follow-up. The user decides in the app —
queue it as a slice, fold it into this one, or drop it — and the decision
arrives here as a message naming what to fold in. Do that, then hand back
as below. `complete-slice` refuses while the decision is outstanding.
Never widen your branch to include a follow-up on your own, and never
write them into the summary or the brief instead. No follow-ups: hand
back straight away.

If what you changed is visible — a pane, a page, a rendered component —
and the project already has a cheap or usual way to render it (a gallery
story, a screenshot script, a storybook), render the result and hand the
images in before `complete-slice`:

    nat slice-visuals 3b738308-f654-8170-8c99-eccab4463d8f --project 3b738308-f654-8117-9f2e-c3b0a9b7f001 \
        --visual '<what it shows, one line>
<absolute path to the image>'

`--visual` repeats, one per image. Hand in the full set each time: a later
hand-in replaces an earlier one. Do not build a way to render when the
project has none — go on without images instead. The user reviews them
in the app; their comments, if any, arrive here as a message.

On completion, record the outcome:

    nat complete-slice 3b738308-f654-8170-8c99-eccab4463d8f --project 3b738308-f654-8117-9f2e-c3b0a9b7f001 \
        --branch <branch> --summary '- <what changed>\n- <key decision>' \
        --pr-description '<title line>

<what the PR does and why>'

That records the branch you pushed and hands the slice back for review,
writing the summary onto its page. `--summary` is quoted back to a future
agent in its milestone's digest, not read by a person, so keep it a
handful of terse bullet points — what changed and key decisions — never
a narrative of the session. It leaves the slice in progress on purpose —
approving it in the app's Diff tab is what opens the pull request and
marks it Done.

`--pr-description` is what that pull request is opened with: its first
line is the title and the rest the body, so write it ready to publish —
what the change does and why, for whoever reviews it on GitHub, not a
report of your session. Pass `--pr-description -` to read it from stdin
when it is too long for an argument, and give `--summary` as a flag then.

Make no unverifiable claims in either one: say only what you actually
checked, never what you assume or expect to be true. "nothing invented"
about a value you interpolated rather than read is exactly the kind of
line that costs somebody else an hour redoing the work to find out it
was wrong.

Leave `--branch` off when the slice produced no branch — a docs or
research slice — and it is marked Done there and then, with no pull
request to describe. A summary too long for one argument can be piped in
on stdin instead of passing `--summary`.

If you cannot complete it, say what stopped you and leave the slice
in progress, so nobody else picks it up on top of your work:

    nat complete-slice 3b738308-f654-8170-8c99-eccab4463d8f --project 3b738308-f654-8117-9f2e-c3b0a9b7f001 \
        --blocked --summary '<what is blocking>'

